<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret Thatcher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret Thatcher was a British Conservative politician and the first woman prime minister of the United Kingdom, serving from 1979 to 1990. Her governments privatised state industries, curbed trade union power, deregulated finance and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a> in 1982; her policies, known as Thatcherism, reshaped the British economy and divided opinion deeply.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher.jpg" alt="Margaret Thatcher" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Bogaerts · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_Thatcher_(1983).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret Hilda Roberts was born on 13 October 1925 in Grantham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>. Her father, Alfred Roberts, owned a tobacconist's and a grocery shop, served as an alderman and was a Methodist local preacher; he brought her up as a strict Wesleyan Methodist. She won a scholarship to Kesteven and Grantham Girls' School and entered Somerville College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in October 1943 to study chemistry.</p><p>In 1950 and 1951 she was the Conservative candidate for the Labour seat of Dartford, losing both times. She married Denis Thatcher in December 1951 and qualified as a barrister in 1953. She was elected Member of Parliament for Finchley in 1959. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-heath">Edward Heath</a> appointed her Secretary of State for Education and Science in 1970, and in 1975 she defeated him in the Conservative Party leadership election, becoming the first woman to lead a major British political party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Thatcher became prime minister on 4 May 1979. Her economic policies emphasised individual liberty, the privatisation of state-owned companies and reducing the power of trade unions. Her popularity waned during the recession and rising unemployment, but victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a> in 1982 brought a resurgence of support and a landslide victory in 1983. In 1984 she survived the Provisional IRA's bombing of a Brighton hotel, and she achieved a political victory against the National Union of Mineworkers in the strike of 1984–85. In 1986 she oversaw the deregulation of financial markets known as the Big Bang.</p><p>She won a third term in 1987. Her support for the Community Charge, also called the poll tax, was widely unpopular, and her increasingly Eurosceptic views were not shared by others in her cabinet. After a challenge to her leadership she resigned in 1990 and was succeeded by John Major.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Thatcher retired from the House of Commons in 1992 and became Baroness Thatcher with a life peerage. She died of a stroke at the Ritz Hotel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 8 April 2013, aged 87. Thatcherism was a systematic overhaul of the post-war consensus on Keynesianism, the welfare state, nationalised industry and high taxes. During her tenure the number of adults owning shares rose from 7 per cent to 25 per cent, and more than a million families bought their council houses. She remains a polarising figure, and the legacy of the shift towards neoliberal policies is still debated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret Thatcher” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Margaret Thatcher</b>'s government called all parties to Lancaster House in late 1979, where three months of talks nearly failed over land reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1979 the governments of <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> privatised state industries, curbed trade unions and deregulated the City of London, accelerating a shift from manufacturing to services and finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Regional leaders could speak for themselves: during the British miners’ strike of 1984–85, when Wałęsa praised <b>Margaret Thatcher</b>, David Jastrzębski, president of Upper Silesia Solidarity, declared his support for the striking miners.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The post-war boom ended in the late 1960s and early 1970s with stagflation, and monetarism gained prominence under <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> (1979–1990) and Ronald Reagan (1981–1989), shifting attention from Keynes’s collectivist concerns to individual choice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1980s <b>Margaret Thatcher</b>’s government privatised large parts of the British economy, and the Reagan administration adopted neoliberalism, bringing 1980s growth and trillion-dollar deficits.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>After the economic crisis of the 1970s, <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> and Ronald Reagan turned from Keynesian economics to what became known as neoliberalism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in 2, 4 And 6, North Parade, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in The Ritz London, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.92,-0.65&amp;year=1982" aria-label="2, 4 And 6, North Parade on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4536 341 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Margaret Thatcher"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 611h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 611h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.92,-0.65&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
